    meditation: `You cannot remember reciting it. That is the strange thing. You can remember the room — the courthouse rotunda, or the law school auditorium, or the bar association hall with the carpet that smelled of old water. You can remember the chair the judge sat in, the family in the second row, the suit you wore for the photograph afterward that you now find a little too eager in the cut of its shoulders. You can remember the rented robe of the lawyer who moved your admission, and that her voice trembled when she said your name. What you cannot remember, when you try to retrieve it now, is the words you spoke.

That is not a defect of your memory. It is a feature of how oaths work. The oath is taken once and lives afterward as a kind of background hum. You did not memorize it the way you memorized the rule against perpetuities. You repeated it after a judge, in a room of strangers, with your right hand raised, and then you sat down and your professional life began. The words were the doorway. You walked through. Now you cannot reconstruct the doorway, and yet the room you are standing in is on the other side of it.

The bar oath is not the same in every jurisdiction. Yours probably had something like this in it: that you would support the constitutions of the United States and of your state; that you would maintain the respect due to courts of justice and judicial officers; that you would employ for the purpose of maintaining the causes confided to you such means only as are consistent with truth and honor, and would never seek to mislead the judge or jury by any artifice or false statement of fact or law; that you would maintain the confidence and preserve inviolate the secrets of your client. Read it slowly now, on a Wednesday morning, when the brief is half-written and the deposition is at two. Each of those clauses is a knife. You promised, on the threshold of the profession, to refuse the things you have spent a portion of your career being asked, gently and not so gently, to do.

Blackstone, who took oaths more seriously than most modern lawyers ever will, calls the oath the "highest and most awful obligation that human nature can possibly contract." Notice the word awful — it carries its older meaning, full of awe. The oath is awful because it is the act by which a person volunteers his own credibility to the God who made truth possible at all. To swear is to invoke. To swear falsely is, Blackstone says with characteristic directness, to "deny the being of that God whom he invoked to attest his words." You did not, that day in the rotunda, simply make a professional commitment. You called on the Name. You said, in effect: by the One who is the truth, let this be my word.

This is the part that ought to wake you up. You did this. You raised your hand and said the words and the words were heard. The hearing did not require your continuous attention afterward, and the oath did not lapse because you stopped thinking about it. It is still operative. Every appearance you have entered, every filing you have signed, every retainer you have undertaken — all of it has been done within the running force of that single invocation. The professional structure of your life is the structure of an oath being kept or broken in small motions every week.

Psalm 14 names the kind of person who can dwell on the Lord's holy hill. Among the marks: "He that sweareth to his neighbour, and deceiveth not." The psalm assumes oaths are a normal feature of moral life. It does not denounce them. It only asks whether the one who has sworn is, in fact, the kind of man whose word the Lord can rest upon. The oath does not make him so. The oath presupposes that he is so, and the oath then commits him publicly to remaining so. The lawyer's oath does the same. It does not bestow integrity. It assumes you brought some, and it binds what you brought.

The danger of the bar oath is that it is recited once and never recited again. There is no annual renewal. No moment, formally, when the judge calls you back into the rotunda and asks you to say it through again. Compare this with the religious life. The Benedictine renews his profession. The married couple renews vows at the anniversary, sometimes badly, sometimes well. Even the citizen, on the right civic occasions, is given an opportunity to put his hand over his heart and pledge again. The lawyer is given nothing. He must construct his own renewal, in private, or the oath becomes a relic. A relic is the wrong relationship to it. The oath is alive. It is binding you right now. The question is whether you remember.

If you can find your jurisdiction's oath text, print it tonight. Read it aloud, slowly, alone. Read it again on the first morning of every quarter. Read it, especially, on the day you are tempted to do what you swore you would not do. The witness's hand goes on the book and his life is altered. Your hand went up in a rotunda once, years ago, and the same thing happened to you. It is too late to take it back. It is, mercifully, not too late to remember what you said.

The lawyer who keeps the oath he cannot remember reciting is the lawyer the psalm is describing. He may not feel especially holy. He may not even feel especially competent. He is, however, a man whose word the Lord rests upon. He swore. He has not deceived. The tabernacle is, more than he knows, his to dwell in.`,
