    meditation: `The lawyer reading Matthew 5 has a problem. The Lord, in the Sermon on the Mount, appears to forbid the very thing the lawyer does for a living. "But I say to you not to swear at all." If the lawyer takes this literally, he cannot file an affidavit. He cannot administer the oath at deposition. He cannot place his hand on the book to be sworn at his own admission. The whole apparatus of testimony, on which the law is constructed, collapses into sin.

This is not the right reading. It has never been the right reading. Augustine, Jerome, Chrysostom, Aquinas, the Catholic tradition entire — they all read Matthew 5 as not abolishing oaths but as deepening the standard for them. Paul himself swears in his letters, not perjuriously. The Lord in his own trial answers under oath when the high priest adjures him by the living God. He does not invoke his own Sermon to refuse. He answers, "Thou hast said it." The tradition reads the Sermon, therefore, not as banning the form, but as exposing what the form is for and what it costs to use lightly.

What is the Lord doing in Matthew 5, then? He is doing what he does throughout the Sermon. He is taking a moral precept that the rabbinic culture of his day had domesticated and restoring it to its terrifying original force. The old law said: do not forswear yourself, perform your oaths to the Lord. The teachers of the time had built around this an elaborate casuistry of which oaths counted and which were loopholes — swearing by Jerusalem versus by the temple, by the altar versus by the gift on the altar, and so on. The Lord cuts through the whole architecture. Every oath is on the Lord, because the cosmos is on him. Heaven is his throne. Earth is his footstool. Your own head, of which you cannot turn one hair, belongs to him before it belongs to you. There is nothing left to swear by that is not already his. The casuistry was always self-deception. You were always invoking him.

The Lord's positive teaching is more demanding than the prohibition. "Let your speech be yea, yea: no, no: and that which is over and above these, is of evil." He is calling for a kind of person whose ordinary speech does not require oath-reinforcement, because his ordinary speech is already true. The reason a man needs to swear is that his unsworn word has been found unreliable. Swear strengthens what your reputation has weakened. The Lord is asking, in effect: live as the kind of person whose unsworn word would be enough.

Augustine, preaching to ordinary Christians who were under daily pressure to swear over matters of commerce and litigation, reads the Sermon with the realism of a North African bishop. He does not abolish the oath. He admits, with sober Latin, that "if any necessity of the case shall force thee to swear, let it be from a great cause, and let it be from a true heart." But he locates the danger precisely. "He that uses himself to swear, comes by little and little to perjury." The danger is not the occasional grave oath, taken under judgment, justice, and truth. The danger is habituation. The mouth that swears easily will, eventually, swear falsely, because the form has been emptied by overuse. The man who does not swear, Augustine says, cannot perjure himself. He has not eliminated the possibility of error. He has eliminated the possibility of a particular kind of error — the kind that drags the Lord's name into a falsehood.

For the lawyer, this is the operative reading. You will swear. You will take oaths in court. You will sign verifications. You will administer oaths to your witnesses. The Sermon does not forbid these. The Sermon, read through Augustine, requires you to make sure of two things.

First, that when you do swear, the cause is great and the heart is true. Not every verification needs to be approached as if it were Sinai. But every verification needs to be approached as if you, in fact, were the one verifying — not the paralegal who drafted it, not the rote of the form, not the deadline that pushed it through. The signature is yours. The Lord is invoked. Whatever room you have for routine, you do not have room for unconscious oath-taking.

Second, that your unsworn speech is in such order that the oath, when it comes, adds only the formality, not the weight. The lawyer whose word is trustworthy in the corridor, in the email, in the casual phone call, has already done most of the work Matthew 5 is asking for. The oath he then takes in the courtroom is not strained to carry credibility his ordinary speech did not have. His yea is yea before he swears. His nay is nay. The oath is the ratification of a pattern, not an attempt to compensate for the lack of one.

This is the harder reading, because the easier one is just to bracket Matthew 5 as a hyperbolic image and go on as before. The harder reading asks you to consider how much oath-strengthening your daily speech relies on. Do you say "to be honest with you" because, in your ordinary speech, you have not been? Do you reach for "I swear" or "I promise" as conversational fillers, the residue of a habit Augustine warned against in literal terms? Do you treat the verification at the bottom of a pleading as different in kind from the rest of the pleading, or do you treat the rest of the pleading as if it were already, in some sense, sworn? The Lord is asking, through the lawyer's profession, that his disciple inhabit a single register. Your sworn speech and your unsworn speech are the same speech, because the One who hears them is the same.

The Sermon does not forbid the affidavit. It forbids the gap between the man under oath and the man not under oath. Close the gap, and the lawyer can swear without contradiction. Keep the gap, and even the unsworn word becomes a small perjury.`,
