    meditation: `The affidavit is the daily oath. The lawyer who took the bar oath once will sign a hundred affidavits in a year, two hundred, more. The form has been routinized to the point of near-invisibility. There is a paragraph at the end, typed in the same font as everything else, that says you swear under penalty of perjury that the foregoing is true and correct. You sign. The paralegal notarizes. The document is filed.

Blackstone's sentence ought to embarrass us. "All evidence ought to be given upon oath, that is, upon a religious and solemn appeal to God." Religious and solemn. The phrase is not decorative. He means that every affidavit you sign is, structurally, an act of religion. You are appealing to God to bear witness to what you have written. The court accepts the document because of this appeal. Without the appeal — without the invocation, however attenuated in our procedural language — the document would be, in Blackstone's striking phrase, "of no more validity than the loose talk of the populace in the streets." It is the oath that makes the difference between an affidavit and a rumor.

This is worth holding. The affidavit you signed last Tuesday at 4:43 PM, on the way out the door, with the paralegal hovering because the courier had to leave at five — that affidavit was a religious act. It used the Name. The Name was heard. The Name did not consult your level of attention before agreeing to the hearing. The metaphysics ran whether or not you noticed.

Ecclesiasticus warns the careless swearer in language no modern jurisdiction would print on the affidavit form. "As a slave daily put to the question, is never without a blue mark: so every one that sweareth, and nameth, shall not be wholly pure from sin." The image is of a man being repeatedly beaten — the daily oath as a daily wound. The wisdom literature understood, with a directness our procedural culture has lost, that habituation to swearing leaves marks. The marks are not on the paper. They are on the soul. The lawyer who has signed three thousand affidavits without serious attention is not a more practiced lawyer; he is, in the older language, a more bruised one. The wounds have come quietly. He no longer feels them. The accumulation is what the scripture is warning him about.

What does it look like to take the affidavit seriously? Several practical things.

It looks like reading the document, in full, before you sign. Not skimming. Not trusting the associate who drafted it. Reading. If the document is long, scheduling time to read it. If the case is large enough to generate a thirty-page affidavit, the case is large enough to deserve forty-five minutes of your undivided attention before you swear to it. If the firm's culture does not accommodate forty-five minutes, the firm is asking you to sin in a routinized way and calling it efficiency.

It looks like asking what the document actually says. Lawyers are professionally trained to write affidavits in a register that is technically true and rhetorically convenient. There is space between those two. The affidavit can be technically true and yet built to give the reader an impression that is, in the relevant sense, false. The deponent did not say X, but the affidavit, by including only sentences Y and Z and omitting sentence W, leads the reader to believe X. You drafted it. You read it. The signature is the moment to ask whether the document is honest or only technically defensible. Aquinas's truth — veritas — is not satisfied by technical defensibility. It is satisfied by what the document is built to make the reader think.

It looks like noticing the verification clause as a clause. Read the verification aloud to yourself before signing. "I declare under penalty of perjury that the foregoing is true and correct to the best of my knowledge and belief." Each phrase is a hinge. To the best of your knowledge — have you actually checked, or are you trusting what the client said without verifying what was verifiable? To the best of your belief — have you formed a belief, or are you signing as a placeholder? The clause is asking you something specific. The lawyer who signs without answering the clause's question has, again, not actually sworn. He has performed the form of swearing while exempting himself from its content.

It looks like asking your client to do the same. If the affidavit is the client's, your job is to make sure he has done what you would do. Lawyers tend to walk clients through affidavits in a way that protects the lawyer's deadline more than the client's conscience. We say: "I'll send you the affidavit Friday morning; please review it and let me know if anything needs to change before we file at noon." We have given the client three hours, and we have given him a document drafted in our voice, and we have not actually asked him to do what the affidavit will be doing on his behalf — to swear before God to its truth. He will sign. He may not have sworn. The form was performed. The metaphysics, again, runs.

Notice that Blackstone grounds the credit of all human testimony in this one act. The court system, for him, is constructed on the assumption that the deponent believes in a God who hears. Without that belief, the entire architecture of evidence is, by his lights, "loose talk." This is the Christian inheritance of the common law, and it is largely forgotten. Most lawyers today would not articulate the theology of the affidavit, and many witnesses do not believe in the God whose name the form invokes. The law still trusts the form. The form was built to bind the conscience of a believer. When the believer signs it, the form is doing what it was built to do. When a non-believer signs it, the form is doing something it was not built to do — relying on the penalty of perjury alone, without the deeper sanction. The lawyer who is a believer is, on this reading, the only one to whom the affidavit form is fully addressed. The form was written for him. It is asking him to be what the form assumes.

The lawyer takes the bar oath once. He takes the daily oath every time he signs. The first oath is the doorway. The daily oath is the floor. The floor is the part you stand on every working day. Notice it. Walk on it as if you knew what it was made of.`,
