    meditation: `You watched a friend take the oath of office. Or you watched a stranger, on television, the morning of an inauguration; or you watched a newly appointed judge in your own circuit, in a courtroom packed for the occasion. The friend or stranger raised the right hand. The chief justice or senior judge read the words. The newly invested public servant repeated them. The room applauded. The robe was put on, or the badge pinned, or the gavel handed across the bench. Photographs were taken. A reception followed. The order of business proceeded.

What just happened? In the language of Deuteronomy, a man or woman just accepted the judgment of God as a personal commission. "There shall be no difference of persons, you shall hear the little as well as the great." This is the substance of the judicial oath in every common-law jurisdiction descended from English practice. It is also the substance of the oath of office for government lawyers, attorneys general, prosecutors, and the rest of the public legal apparatus. They bind themselves, before God and before the people, to do equal right. The words are different in different places. The substance is older than all the words.

Blackstone, with characteristic Anglican gravity, calls the oath of office "not a ceremony, but the substance of the trust." Notice what he is doing. He is refusing the separation we have learned to make between the form and the content. He insists that the form is the content. The oath is not the wrapper around the substance of the office. The oath is the office. The judge holds the bench because he has sworn what he has sworn. The judge who departs from the oath, Blackstone says, has not merely broken a promise — he has, "by his own deed," renounced the obligation. The magistracy, "though it be not formally vacated, is in conscience already forfeit."

This is a strong sentence and it should be allowed to be strong. The judge whose rulings systematically depart from his oath has, in Blackstone's reading, ceased to hold office in the deepest sense, even while continuing to exercise its powers. He has not been impeached. The seal still bears his signature. The marshal still calls his court to order. But the inner office — the one that was constituted by the oath he took — is empty. He is wearing a robe over the absence of an office. The same is true, mutatis mutandis, of the attorney general who acts in defiance of his commission, the prosecutor who indicts at the pleasure of a political patron, the public lawyer who treats the office as a platform.

The oath of office is a particular case of the more general law of oaths, but it carries a freight peculiar to itself. The bar oath you took once binds you in private practice; the oath of office binds you in public trust. The difference is not in the gravity. Both are total. The difference is in the audience and the matter. The bar oath binds your practice toward your clients and the courts. The oath of office binds your practice toward the people, who do not know you, will never meet you, and will be affected by your decisions in ways you will never trace. The judge who hears the eviction case at nine has, in some sense, been sworn for the tenant he has not yet seen. The prosecutor who reviews the file at his desk has been sworn for the family of a victim he will never address. The oath reaches further than the swearing-in itself could see.

If you have ever been on the cusp of judicial appointment, or have been nominated for a public role, or are weighing whether to put your name in for one — sit with the oath you would be taking. Print it. Read it slowly. Ask whether you can say it without reservation. Ask whether you can say it without crossing a finger behind your back at the clause about doing equal right to the poor as to the rich. Ask whether your record, were it produced in evidence on the day of the ceremony, would corroborate or contradict what you would be about to swear. The oath of office presupposes a person who can say it honestly. It does not bestow such a person. If you cannot say the oath honestly, the right answer is to decline the office. The decline is not a failure; it is a refusal to begin a public life by perjury. Many public lives have begun by perjury, and the country has paid the cost. Do not be among them.

Deuteronomy adds a phrase the modern oaths often omit but the older mind always assumed: "And if any thing seem hard to you, refer it to me." The judge in Israel was not, on the strength of his appointment, the final court. There was a higher court, and the higher court was available. The oath was sworn into a system in which appeal was possible — not procedural appeal, but spiritual appeal, the petition of the judge himself, in private, to the God whose judgment he was administering. The modern judge has lost much of this practice. There are conferences. There are bench books. There are colleagues. There is no longer, in most courthouses, a tradition of the judge praying before the bench in any sustained way. The interior structure of the office — the part Blackstone called the substance — has been thinned. The form remains. The form, by itself, will not bear the weight of the cases that come.

If you are a friend of a newly appointed judge or government lawyer, your work is to remember the oath with them. Not to lecture. Not to invigilate. To remember. To be the kind of friend at whose table the new judge can come, six months in, and admit that the easy part — the swearing-in, the photographs — has been replaced by the hard part, which is the daily living of what was sworn. To be the friend who will pray for them, by name, on the morning of the hard ruling. To be, in a small way, what Moses was to the judges of Israel: the one who said, "if any thing seem hard to you, refer it to me," and meant it.

The oath of office is not a ceremony. It is the form of a life, made public, before God. The judge who keeps it is the substance of the trust. The judge who does not is, in Blackstone's quiet and devastating sentence, already forfeit, even as the gavel still answers to his hand.`,
    prayer: `Lord, you who judged in Israel through judges, and judge us still through those who have sworn to do equal right, hold them to the substance of their oath. Where they have wavered, recall them. Where they are tempted, steady them. Where I have a friend at the bench, give me the courage to be the table at which the hard ruling can be brought. And let me never accept an office whose oath I could not say in your hearing.`,
