    meditation: `There is an oath in the Christian tradition that was not taken. The Oath of Supremacy, in the form put before Thomas More in April 1534, required him to swear that Henry VIII was the supreme head on earth of the Church in England. More had been Lord Chancellor of that England a few years earlier. He had defended the King's interests in matters where his conscience permitted. He had drawn the line at points others had failed to draw it. Now the King's commissioners came to Lambeth, and the oath was put before him, and More — who loved his life, loved his daughter, loved his garden at Chelsea, loved the King who had walked arm in arm with him there — declined to swear.

The decision did not produce a denunciation. It did not produce a sermon. It produced, in the first instance, only a refusal, accompanied by the quiet sentence Roper records: "I will not meddle with what oath others may take, nor will I judge any man's conscience save my own." More refused to swear, and he refused to condemn those who had sworn. He treated his conscience as a matter between himself and God, and he treated the consciences of others as matters between themselves and God. He did not turn his refusal into a campaign. He simply refused, and waited.

This is the part of More that the lawyer most needs and most often misreads. The drama of the scaffold is real. The line on the platform — "the King's good servant, but God's first" — is real. But the substance of More's witness was not the scaffold. The substance was the conversation in the Tower with Margaret, and the dozens of small refusals to make his conscience into anyone else's argument. He did not become a polemicist. He became, more deeply, a lawyer keeping faith with the metaphysics of the oath form he had spent his life respecting.

Aquinas, as we saw earlier in the week, taught that an oath to do an unlawful thing does not bind. The principle freed More. The oath the commissioners offered required him to certify a falsehood — that the King was supreme head of the Church in England, when the Church had a head elsewhere. More could have sworn and rationalized. Many did. Some had reasons that were not contemptible. Most had reasons that were. More refused, on the simple ground that his oath would be a perjury — not because the form was defective, but because the content was false in a matter on which he was being asked to invoke God. The God whose name was about to be used was the same God whose Church was about to be denied. The oath would have asked God to bear witness against himself. That oath, More understood, could not be sworn.

The letter to Margaret, sent from the Tower, is what the lawyer should sit with this week. "I think no man living, that hath not so good cause as I, would be more loth to lose his life." He is not pretending to enjoy what is coming. He is not affecting indifference. He loves his life. He loves his daughter to whom he writes. He acknowledges that without his cause — without the specific demand of his conscience in this specific case — there is no man more reluctant than he to walk to the block. Then the hinge: "But yet, for the safeguard of my soul, I dare not swear." Dare not. The verb is precise. He is not refusing because he disagrees with the King. He is refusing because he is afraid of God in a register that has gone almost entirely out of our culture. He dare not. The cost of swearing is too high, not in terms of consequence, but in terms of the soul whose safeguard is at stake.

This is where the lawyer's vocational life intersects with the martyr's. Most lawyers will not be asked to die for an oath they cannot swear. Most lawyers will be asked, however, to sign documents whose content they know to be a lesser kind of lie. The retainer that papers over a falsehood. The settlement that whitewashes an unjust act. The affidavit that omits the inconvenient fact. The certification at the bottom of a regulatory filing that is technically true but materially deceiving. Each of these is, in miniature, the same form. The conscience says: I cannot sign this and remain who I am before God. The pressure says: but everyone signs these; the consequences of refusing are large; you will lose the case, the client, the partnership, the appointment. More's daughter, in the Tower, made versions of these arguments to her father in a way that no client of yours has matched for moral weight. More heard them. He did not yield. He was right not to yield. The form of his rightness is the form available to you in the smaller cases.

Acts gives the apostolic ground. The high priest commands the apostles not to teach. They answer: "We ought to obey God, rather than men." The sentence is not a slogan for civil disobedience as political strategy. It is a sentence about the order of obediences. When two authorities conflict, the lower yields. The lower may take your life. It cannot take the order. The apostles, like More, lost the immediate case. The Sanhedrin won the courtroom. The apostles won the verdict that mattered, and the church is still here.

The lawyer's question, when conscience and oath collide, is therefore not "what is prudent." It is "what is the order of my obediences." If the oath asks what cannot be given without unfaith to the God in whose name the oath would be sworn, the oath cannot be sworn. The cost will follow, and may be large. It will be smaller than the cost of having used the divine name to certify a falsehood.

A practical word, because More is too easy to admire and too hard to follow. When you sense a coming collision — the firm wants a brief you cannot in good conscience write; the regulator wants a certification you cannot honestly give; the public body wants an oath whose content you cannot affirm — do not wait for the crisis to formulate the refusal. Formulate it now, in writing, to yourself. Write the sentence you would say. Read it to a Christian colleague, or to your priest, or to your spouse. The crisis is the moment of utterance, not of composition. Compose now. When the moment comes, your refusal will already have taken shape, and you will not be improvising at the door of the room where the oath is offered.

The Oath of Supremacy still exists, in many forms, in many rooms. Most of them are smaller than Lambeth. The principle is the same. There is an oath that cannot be sworn without perjury, and the refusal to swear it is the strongest oath you will take in your life.`,
