    meditation: `The week ends, as oath weeks should, not in the courthouse but in the kitchen. You sat across from a friend last month and said you would do something for him. You told a colleague, in the corridor after a meeting, that you would have the memo to her by Thursday. You said to your son, in the car on the way to school, that you would be home for the game on Saturday. None of these were oaths in the formal sense. There was no book under your hand. There was no commissioner watching. There was, in James's striking phrase, no swearing by heaven or earth. There was only your word, in private, given casually, and received.

Augustine warned that the man who uses himself to swearing comes by little and little to perjury. James, more bluntly, says: do not swear at all. Let your yea be yea and your nay be nay, that you fall not under judgment. The teaching is the same teaching the Lord gave in Matthew 5, repeated by his brother in the apostolic church a generation later. It cannot be a coincidence that James puts it in his letter "above all things." Above the trials he has just been discussing. Above the patience under affliction. Above the prayers of the elders for the sick. Above all things — your word should be reliable enough that you do not need to reinforce it with an oath at all.

This is the test of the lawyer's interior life, more than the bar oath itself, more than the affidavits, more than the oath of office. The interior life of the lawyer is measured by what his ordinary, unsworn word is worth. The bar oath is a public form. The affidavit is a procedural form. The oath of office is an institutional form. All of them have witnesses, penalties, archives. Your word in private has none of these. Nobody is going to file your conversation in the corridor. There is no docket for your promise to your son. The only sanction on your unsworn word is the kind of person you are. James is telling you that this is the relevant sanction. The God who hears your sworn word also hears every other word, and he does not switch on his hearing when the book comes out.

Newman writes about this with the affectionate precision of a pastor who has seen too many people fail at it. "He who is in the practice of being true in his common words and dealings, has no need to be told, when occasion comes, that he must speak the truth. His habit will lead him to it of itself." The keeping of an oath is not a separate skill from the keeping of a private word. It is the same skill, exercised in two registers. The lawyer who can be trusted in court is the lawyer who can already be trusted at home. The lawyer who is trustworthy under penalty is, in the end, the lawyer who was trustworthy without one. The oath is the public confirmation of a private habit. It does not manufacture the habit. It assumes it.

The inverse is the warning. The lawyer who breaks his private word easily — the promise to call back, the promise to be home, the promise to look at the friend's matter pro bono — is in training to break a larger one. Not because the break will scale up in some dramatic way, but because the muscle has been formed. The Greek pseudos is the same in the casual lie and in the sworn one. The court's penalty differs. The God who hears does not differ. By the time you arrive at the witness stand, or the affidavit, or the oath of office, you are already either the kind of man who keeps his word or the kind who does not. The form will reveal which.

Aristotle and Aquinas both observed that virtue is built by habituation. You do not become temperate in a moment of crisis. You become temperate by a thousand small choices accumulated into a settled disposition. The same is true of truthfulness. You do not become a man whose oath can be trusted by gripping the book hard enough in the courtroom. You become him by being the kind of man whose unsworn word at lunch was already true, whose promise to his daughter at bedtime was already kept, whose offhand commitment to a colleague was already discharged on time. The oath, when it comes, is then a small motion. You are already saying what you would have said.

There is a specific lawyer's danger here. We are professionally fluent. We can construct sentences that are technically true and rhetorically misleading, and we can do this so quickly that we do it before our conscience objects. The fluency is a gift of the trade and a moral hazard. The lawyer who lives in his fluency long enough begins to hedge with his spouse the way he hedges in a memo, to frame his answer to his friend the way he frames a response to opposing counsel. The yea, yea, no, no — the simple speech the apostle calls for — becomes harder, not easier, with seniority. The senior partner who can no longer say a plain sentence to his wife is the senior partner whose word in court has also begun, quietly, to be hedged.

So end the week here. End it not with the dramatic oath of More, or the public oath of office, or the procedural oath of the affidavit, but with the oath you took yesterday and called something else. The promise to a friend over coffee. The commitment to your child. The text to your colleague at 9:14 PM saying you would have it done by morning. These are the practice ground of your sworn word. The God who heard More in the Tower hears you in the corridor. The God who hears the witness on the stand hears you in the kitchen.

Newman's last sentence is the one to carry into next week. "His sworn speech is only the ordinary speech put on, as it were, before a particular witness whose presence he has never doubted." This is the standard. Your sworn speech and your ordinary speech are the same speech. The witness is the same witness. The form is sometimes louder. The hearing is the same hearing. The lawyer who lives as if this were true is the lawyer who keeps his oaths, because he has been keeping them all along, before anyone administered them, in the kitchen, in the car, in the small obligations that nobody filed.

That is the oath the week has been building toward. Not the bar oath, in its way. Not the affidavit, though it matters. Not even the oath of office, though to some of you it will come and you must keep it. The deepest oath is the one you take a hundred times a day without naming it as one. Keep that, and the rest will keep themselves.`,
    prayer: `Lord, you who hear every word, sworn and unsworn, the same hearing — make my private speech the practice ground of my public oaths. Let my yea be yea in the kitchen and at the bench. Where I have grown fluent at the cost of being plain, restore my plainness. And let the God who heard your servants in the Tower hear me also at home, where the smaller oaths are taken, and kept, or quietly broken in the dark.`,
